cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "transport-ingest-python: the fabric's unreliable WebTransport half, implemented a second time"
abstract: >
  A second implementation of the unreliable half of the V-Sekai multiplayer fabric's
  WebTransport contract, on pywebtransport rather than picoquic. It carries QUIC
  datagrams of back-to-back 100-byte entity records with no framing, recovers the
  count by division, drops a datagram whose length is not a multiple of the record
  size, and hands the records off over the iceoryx2 ring. It exists to disagree
  with transport-ingest-c where one of the two is wrong about the contract, which a
  single implementation cannot reveal, and it carries no player traffic.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/transport-ingest-python"
keywords:
  - webtransport
  - http3
  - quic
  - iceoryx2
  - interoperability
  - conformance

# What this is built on. A second implementation is defined by what it refuses to read as much
# as by what it depends on, so the contract repositories are listed beside the libraries: the
# specifications are the input, and the C implementation is deliberately not one.
references:
  - type: software
    title: "pywebtransport: an async-native WebTransport stack for Python"
    authors:
      - name: "The pywebtransport authors"
    year: 2026
    version: 0.20.1
    license: Apache-2.0
    url: "https://github.com/wtransport/pywebtransport"
    notes: >
      The QUIC and HTTP/3 stack this terminates on. Its core is Rust and shares no
      code with picoquic, which is the property that makes this a second opinion
      rather than a second copy.

  - type: software
    title: "Eclipse iceoryx2: lock-free zero-copy inter-process communication"
    authors:
      - name: "Eclipse iceoryx2 contributors"
    year: 2026
    version: 0.9.3
    license: Apache-2.0
    url: "https://github.com/eclipse-iceoryx/iceoryx2"
    notes: >
      The ring. Reached here through the prebuilt Python wheel rather than the
      dlsym stub table the C repositories generate from iceoryx2.sigs; RFD 0123
      records that departure.

  - type: software
    title: "contract-entity-packet: the 100-byte integral entity wire packet, proved"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/lean-entity-packet"
    notes: >
      The layout, the golden vectors, and the emitter that writes gen/. The reason
      no file here carries a field offset.

  - type: software
    title: "contract-wt: the second opinion, in aioquic"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/contract-wt"
    notes: >
      The same argument one stack earlier. It checks the engine's H3/WT from the
      RFCs on aioquic, and its roster client is what a live cross-test here uses.

  - type: standard
    title: "RFC 9297: HTTP Datagrams and the Capsule Protocol"
    authors:
      - family-names: Schinazi
        given-names: David
      - family-names: Pauly
        given-names: Tommy
    year: 2022
    url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9297.html"
    notes: "Read instead of the C implementation, which is the point."

  - type: standard
    title: "RFC 9114: HTTP/3"
    authors:
      - family-names: Bishop
        given-names: Mike
    year: 2022
    url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html"
    notes: "The Extended CONNECT this terminates."
